issues.body:
Removing a member with `DELETE` requires the `GroupMembership` id. A push-based client often knows only the group and the member, so every removal needs a filtered GET first.

Change: add non-normative guidance after the DELETE section:
- a client can find the membership by filtering on both `group.value` and `member.value` (see the related filtering change);
- clients that create memberships with POST can keep the id from the response and avoid that lookup;
- if the service provider supports modifying `Group.members`, a client can instead send a PATCH `remove` with a `members[value eq "..."]` path to the Group, as in RFC7644 Section 3.5.2.2, which needs no membership id.

https://github.com/Zollnerd/scim-group-membership/blob/7d2bee0bd3d443a629d6564b8c8ed23f94fb3907/draft-zollner-scim-group-membership.md?plain=1#L314-L319

The draft says every attribute's `mutability` becomes `readOnly` if the service provider doesn't support creating or deleting memberships. That condition also catches service providers that support only one of the two, where `readOnly` is wrong. `mutability` describes attributes, not which operations are supported, and the draft doesn't say what error an unsupported operation returns.

Change:
- apply the `readOnly` rule only when the service provider supports neither create nor delete;
- require `501 Not Implemented` (RFC7644 Section 3.12) for unsupported POST or DELETE;
- note that `403 Forbidden` is the response when the operation is supported but not permitted for the requesting client.

https://github.com/Zollnerd/scim-group-membership/blob/7d2bee0bd3d443a629d6564b8c8ed23f94fb3907/draft-zollner-scim-group-membership.md?plain=1#L111

The draft requires pagination and calls index-based pagination "primary" and cursors an "alternative". It doesn't say which method a client can count on or how the client finds out. At the scale this draft targets, index-based pagination is the weaker choice: RFC7644 Section 3.4.2.4 says clients MUST be prepared for inconsistent results when the data changes between pages. The cursor example also doesn't show how a cursor query starts. RFC9865 Section 2 starts with an empty `cursor` parameter and continues with each response's `nextCursor`.

Change:
- require at least one method;
- recommend cursor-based pagination;
- require the methods to be advertised with the RFC9865 Section 4 `pagination` attribute in `/ServiceProviderConfig`;
- remove "primary";
- show both the first and the next cursor request, plus a response containing `nextCursor`.

https://github.com/Zollnerd/scim-group-membership/blob/7d2bee0bd3d443a629d6564b8c8ed23f94fb3907/draft-zollner-scim-group-membership.md?plain=1#L280-L292

The paragraph on unfiltered `GET /GroupMemberships` doesn't say whether a service provider may refuse an unfiltered request. It also doesn't require a stable result order across pages. Finally, it frames internal sorting by `group.value` with a MAY, which looks like a requirement but is really implementation advice.

Change:
- allow a 400 `tooMany` response (RFC7644 Section 3.12) for unfiltered requests, and state that memberships stay available through filtered requests, so this is consistent with the requirement to make every authorized membership available;
- require a stable result order across the pages of a single query;
- reword the sorting sentence as a non-normative example.

